{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MSbtT7SdMD3Xdz4RNRsu8a","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, the experimental plan, the imagined results, and the MVP simulation evidence. Let me compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nContamCal proposes a two-stage post-hoc calibration framework for benchmark scores under training data contamination. Stage 1 aggregates four detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into per-item exposure scores. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model via Bayesian MCMC, yielding calibrated ability estimates with credible intervals. The paper claims 1.97pp MAE with 93% CI coverage on controlled SFT injection experiments, 44% improvement over the best baseline, and 42% regret reduction for RL-trained models.\n\n---\n\n### A. Methodological Soundness\n\n**A1. 4PL+gamma formulation.**\nThe formulation in Eq. (2) is mathematically coherent: adding gamma * e_ji to the ability parameter theta_j inside the logistic link is a standard covariate-in-the-exponent approach from item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017). However, using a **single global gamma** is a strong and potentially problematic assumption. It asserts that one unit of exposure produces the same ability boost regardless of:\n- The model (a 0.5B model memorizing a MMLU fact vs. a 7B model)\n- The item type (MCQ knowledge recall vs. code generation)\n- The benchmark (MMLU vs. HumanEval)\n\nThe paper acknowledges that smaller models show larger inflation (10.8pp vs. 8.2pp at 50% dosage), yet the IRT model forces a single gamma across all models and benchmarks in each fit. If the model is fitted per-benchmark (the paper is ambiguous on this), then the cross-benchmark constraint is relaxed, but per-model heterogeneity in contamination susceptibility remains unmodeled. A hierarchical gamma (e.g., gamma_j ~ N(mu_gamma, sigma_gamma)) or at minimum benchmark-specific gammas would be more defensible. The paper should clarify the scope of each IRT fit and justify why per-model heterogeneity does not bias theta estimates.\n\n**A2. RRF aggregation.**\nThe meta-circularity argument against learned weights (Section 2.1 of the framing) is reasonable but somewhat hand-wavy. Learned weights could be trained on synthetic contamination (which the authors already produce for ground truth), avoiding the circularity concern. The actual reason RRF works well is probably that signal quality is sufficiently uniform in the SFT regime that rank-based fusion is near-optimal. The paper should acknowledge this and note that RRF's advantage is simplicity and robustness to outlier signal scores, not theoretical superiority. The ablation showing calibration-weighted RRF (AUC=0.86) slightly underperforms vanilla RRF (0.87) is interesting but only tested under best-case conditions.\n\n**A3. Priors and identifiability.**\nThe priors are generally reasonable for IRT (LogNormal(0,0.5) for discrimination, Beta(2,8)/(8,2) for guessing/ceiling). The identifiability argument rests on theta-gamma posterior correlation = 0.31, claimed to be below a \"0.4 threshold.\" This threshold appears ad hoc -- I am not aware of a principled identifiability threshold for posterior correlations in IRT models. The MVP simulation results (P2 confounding test) are more convincing: at theta-exposure confounding correlation = 0.38, the residual correlation drops to 0.058 after fitting, and gamma relative bias is 7.7%. However, the MVP was run at a single seed with N_items=300 and N_models=50. The imagined full sweep (exp-004) would strengthen this considerably, but **the paper presents this sweep's results without actually having run it** -- only the single MVP seed exists. This is the most critical gap.\n\n**A4. Measurement error extension.**\nThe formulation e_obs = e_true + epsilon is standard classical measurement error. However, the paper does not specify how e_true is marginalized out in the Stan model. In a proper Bayesian treatment, e_true would be a latent variable for each item, which dramatically increases the parameter space (adding ~N_items latent variables per model). If instead sigma_e is simply used to inflate gamma's posterior variance post-hoc, the uncertainty propagation is approximate and potentially incorrect. The DIC improvement of 12.3 suggests a genuine model, but the implementation details are crucial and missing.\n\n**A5. Counterfactual theta* identification.**\nThe causal claim is that theta_j estimated with gamma*e_ji subtracted represents the \"counterfactual ability\" -- what would be estimated absent contamination. This is valid **only under the assumption that contamination acts purely additively in the logit space and that the exposure scores are calibrated proxies for true exposure**. The additive assumption means contamination does not change the model's actual reasoning ability, only its performance on seen items. This is plausible for SFT memorization but less clear for RL, where training on contaminated data might genuinely improve reasoning. The paper should be more careful in distinguishing \"memorization-inflated performance\" from \"genuine learning from benchmark-adjacent data.\"\n\n---\n\n### B. Experimental Rigor\n\n**B1. Controlled SFT injection protocol.**\nParaphrase-based injection is a well-established protocol (Yang et al. 2023, Oren et al. 2024). The design -- 3 scales x 5 dosages x 4 benchmarks with 15 model pairs -- is solid for internal validity. The key question is ecological validity: real-world contamination involves pre-training data overlap, not post-hoc SFT injection. SFT injection produces a very specific contamination signature (explicit recent memorization during fine-tuning), which may be easier for detection signals to catch than pre-training contamination that has been partially overwritten by subsequent training. The paper should discuss this limitation more explicitly.\n\n**B2. Baselines: the \"---\" problem.**\nThis is a **major presentation weakness** that borders on a rigor concern. Table 4 (the main results table) shows per-benchmark MAE for ContamCal (1.8, 2.1, 1.6, 2.4) but \"---\" for all five baselines across all four benchmarks plus coverage, CI width, and tau. This means:\n- The reader cannot assess whether ContamCal's advantage is uniform across benchmarks or concentrated on a subset\n- Baseline CI coverage and width are unreported, making the uncertainty quantification comparison impossible\n- Kendall tau for baselines is unreported, preventing ranking comparison\n\nThe imagined experiment plan (exp-006) specifies that all baselines should be evaluated on all 5 metrics. The paper as written has **not filled in these numbers**. This would be a fatal flaw at review -- I would immediately desk-reject for incompleteness. Similarly, Tables 5 (per-benchmark detail), 6 (noise), 7 (scale), 11 (few-examinee), and others have extensive \"---\" entries for values that should be computable from the experiments described.\n\n**B3. Evaluation metrics.**\nThe 5 metrics are well-chosen: MAE (calibration), coverage at multiple levels (reliability), CI width (sharpness), Kendall tau (ranking), dose-response Spearman (sanity). The one metric I would add is **calibration slope** -- plot calibrated vs. true scores and measure slope. A method could achieve low MAE through a constant offset while having poor marginal calibration. Also, 50% coverage = 0.55 (from the imagined experiments) suggests slight miscalibration at the 50% level, which should be discussed.\n\n**B4. Scale bridging argument.**\nThe 7B controlled result (MAE=2.2pp) adds incremental confidence. The 72B observational analysis is inevitably weaker -- without ground truth, gamma=0.18 on MMLU could be correct or could reflect systematic bias in the detection signals at this scale. The fact that gamma CI includes zero on GSM8K is presented as \"consistent with reasoning benchmarks being less susceptible,\" but it could equally mean the method has insufficient power at 72B. The argument would be much stronger with a controlled 72B experiment (acknowledged as infeasible due to compute).\n\n**B5. RL case study.**\nThe RL case study is purely observational with 8 models. There is no ground truth for RL contamination, so the 42% regret reduction is computed against... what exactly? The regret calculation requires knowing the \"true\" uncontaminated ranking, but for RL models this is unknown. The paper appears to use the calibrated ranking as a proxy for ground truth when computing regret, which would be circular. If instead regret is computed using downstream task performance or held-out clean benchmarks as ground truth, this should be stated explicitly. As written, this is a significant methodological gap.\n\n**B6. Few-examinee study.**\nN>=50 with pre-estimation from an external pool of 400+ models is a practical but demanding requirement. The pre-estimation strategy effectively assumes that item parameters (a, b, c, d) are stable across different model pools, which may not hold if the evaluation pool has very different capability distributions. The paper should validate this transfer assumption.\n\n---\n\n### C. Presentation Quality\n\n**C1. Tables with \"---\" entries.**\nAs noted in B2, this is the single biggest presentation problem. Of the 14 tables in the paper, approximately 60% of cells that should contain values show \"---\" instead. This makes the paper look like an incomplete draft. The imagined experiments spec shows that all these values were planned to be reported. The effect on reviewers would be devastating -- it signals either that experiments were not actually run, or that unfavorable results are being hidden.\n\n**C2. Missing figures.**\nThe paper contains **zero figures**. The imagined experiment plan describes 21 figures including dose-response curves, scatter plots, heatmaps, bar charts, and coverage calibration plots. None appear in the submitted paper. For a methods paper of this complexity, at minimum these are essential:\n- A framework overview diagram (Stage 1 + Stage 2 pipeline)\n- Calibrated vs. true accuracy scatter plot with CI error bars (the \"money figure\")\n- Signal x regime AUC heatmap\n- Dose-response curves\n\nWithout these, the paper is extremely hard to evaluate visually.\n\n**C3. Writing quality.**\nThe writing is clear, well-organized, and appropriately concise. The introduction effectively motivates the problem and positions the work relative to detection-only and IRT-only approaches. The \"statistical safety net\" framing is compelling. Section 2 (Method) is technically clear. The three structural adaptations from educational IRT to LLM evaluation are well-articulated.\n\n---\n\n### D. Weaknesses and Questions for Authors\n\n**Major weaknesses:**\n\n1. **Incomplete tables (critical).** The overwhelming number of \"---\" entries in results tables makes the paper unacceptable for review in its current form. All baselines must have per-benchmark breakdowns, CI coverage, CI width, and ranking metrics.\n\n2. **No figures (critical).** A paper proposing a complex two-stage Bayesian pipeline with 12 experiments and 14 tables needs at least 4-5 figures. The absence of a single figure is disqualifying.\n\n3. **RL regret calculation may be circular.** How is ground truth defined for the expected regret computation on RL models? If the calibrated scores are used as ground truth, the 42% improvement is tautological.\n\n4. **Single global gamma.** The assumption that contamination has a uniform effect across models and items within a benchmark is strong and potentially violated by the paper's own observation of scale-dependent inflation.\n\n5. **MVP simulation is insufficient for the claims made.** The identifiability argument relies on a single MVP run (1 seed, 1 condition), yet the paper presents results from a comprehensive 144-condition sweep that was planned but may not have been executed. It is unclear which simulation results in the paper are from actual runs vs. imagined best-case outcomes.\n\n**Minor weaknesses:**\n\n6. **Measurement error model implementation details missing.** How are latent true exposure scores handled in the Stan model?\n\n7. **No error bars / standard deviations.** The experiment plan specifies 3 seeds for key experiments, but no standard deviations are reported anywhere in the paper.\n\n8. **Embedding retrieval requires training corpus access.** This is acknowledged implicitly (closed-weight 2-signal mode) but not discussed as a limitation of the full pipeline.\n\n9. **ConStat requires paraphrase generation.** Who generates the paraphrases for ConStat, and could the paraphrase quality itself introduce bias?\n\n**Questions for authors:**\n\nQ1. Is the IRT model fitted jointly across all benchmarks with a single gamma, or separately per benchmark? The paper is ambiguous.\n\nQ2. For the RL expected regret analysis (Table 9), what serves as ground truth for model ranking? How do you know the calibrated ranking is \"better\"?\n\nQ3. The 0.4 threshold for posterior correlation as an identifiability criterion -- what is the theoretical or empirical basis for this specific value?\n\nQ4. How does the measurement error model handle the latent true exposure scores in Stan? Are they fully marginalized, treated as latent variables with MCMC, or handled via an approximation?\n\nQ5. Table 8 (signal x regime) shows embedding retrieval AUC = 0.90 for \"Base\" models. Why would base models (no contamination) show high retrieval AUC? Does this indicate that the embedding signal has high false positive rates on base models?\n\nQ6. The paper reports 50% coverage = 0.55 (from the experiment plan). This exceeds the nominal 50% -- does this indicate the CIs are slightly too wide? Have you considered calibrating the CI widths themselves?\n\n---\n\n### E. Overall Assessment\n\n**Summary judgment:** The core idea is sound and addresses a genuine gap -- no existing method provides calibrated benchmark scores under contamination with proper uncertainty quantification. The cross-disciplinary transfer from educational measurement's item compromise IRT is well-motivated, and the three structural adaptations are non-trivial. The experimental design is comprehensive on paper. However, the submitted manuscript is **clearly incomplete**: pervasive \"---\" entries in tables, zero figures, no error bars, and a potentially circular RL regret calculation. If these issues were resolved, this could be a strong AAAI paper. In its current form, it reads as a detailed experimental plan with a well-written methods section, not a completed study.\n\n**Scores (AAAI 1-10 scale):**\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Overall** | **4/10** | Strong idea, comprehensive plan, but manuscript is incomplete -- missing figures, pervasive \"---\" in tables, no error bars. Cannot evaluate the actual empirical strength. |\n| **Soundness** | **3/5** | Core formulation is correct. Single global gamma, ad hoc identifiability threshold, and potentially circular RL regret are concerns. Measurement error implementation unclear. |\n| **Significance** | **4/5** | High practical value. Contamination calibration with UQ is a genuine unsolved problem. If validated, would be widely adopted. |\n| **Novelty** | **3/5** | Synthesis of existing components (IRT + detection signals + RRF) with meaningful adaptations. The cross-disciplinary transfer is the main novelty. Individual components are not new. |\n| **Clarity** | **2/5** | Writing quality is good, but the manuscript is incomplete. Zero figures and heavily sparse tables make evaluation impossible. |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I am familiar with IRT, Bayesian methods, and LLM contamination literature. My concerns are grounded in methodological standards for this type of work. |\n\n**Recommendation:** Reject in current form. Resubmit after: (1) completing all table entries with actual experimental results and standard deviations, (2) adding at minimum 5 key figures, (3) clarifying the RL regret ground truth, (4) justifying or relaxing the single global gamma assumption, (5) providing measurement error model implementation details. The underlying research direction is promising and could reach acceptance at a top venue with thorough execution."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: ab004b25805546cbf (use SendMessage with to: 'ab004b25805546cbf' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 71876\ntool_uses: 19\nduration_ms: 167250</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"12c88d9d-1477-4077-a4d3-f782256d68e2","uuid":"0bfaca17-2234-43f9-a324-7c24c87c7559","timestamp":"2026-05-13T16:25:23.302Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for an academic peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. Target venue: AAAI 2027 (scale 1-10, acceptance rate ~17.6%).\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Core idea:** A two-stage framework for post-hoc statistical calibration of LLM benchmark scores under training data contamination:\n- Stage 1: Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) of 4 detection signals (embedding retrieval, Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique) → per-item exposure scores\n- Stage 2: Contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model (Bayesian MCMC via Stan NUTS) → calibrated scores with credible intervals\n\n**Key claims:**\n1. ContamCal recovers true performance within 1.97pp MAE with 93% CI coverage (44% improvement over best baseline)\n2. For RL-trained models, produces appropriately wider CIs (11.4pp vs 5.8pp); expected regret analysis shows 42% reduction\n3. Graceful degradation to 2-signal mode for closed-weight models, still beats all baselines by 31%\n\n## Key Method Details (from paper)\n- 4PL IRT equation: P(Y_ij=1) = c_i + (d_i - c_i) / (1 + exp(-a_i(θ_j + γ·e_ji - b_i)))\n- γ is a single global contamination effect parameter; e_ji is per-item exposure score from RRF\n- Priors: θ~N(0,1), γ~N(0,1), a~LogNormal(0,0.5), b~N(0,1), c~Beta(2,8), d~Beta(8,2)\n- Stan NUTS: 4 chains, 2000 warmup, 2000 sampling\n- Noise propagation via measurement error model: e_obs = e_true + ε, ε~N(0,σ_e²)\n\n## Experimental Setup\n- Controlled SFT injection: Qwen2.5 at 0.5B/1.5B/3B × 5 dosage levels (0/5/25/50/100%) × 4 benchmarks (MMLU, GSM8K, ARC-C, HumanEval) = 15 model pairs\n- 7B scale bridging (controlled SFT), 72B observational\n- RL case study: 8 models (Qwen2.5-Instruct, DeepSeek-R1-Distill series)\n- 5 baselines: Remove-rescore, Single-signal 2PL+γ, Vanilla 4PL, Linear regression+CI, Emperor's mitigations\n- 5 metrics: MAE, CI coverage (50/80/95%), CI width, Kendall τ, dose-response Spearman\n\n## Key Results Tables (from paper)\n- Table 4 (main): ContamCal MAE=1.97pp, Cov95=0.93, CI Width=5.8pp, τ=0.91. Best baseline=3.5pp (linear regression)\n- Table 1: Dose-dependent inflation, 9.3pp at 50% on MMLU\n- Table 2: Signal AUCs: Emb=0.88, MinK=0.84, ConStat=0.82, SelfCrit=0.79 (SFT); RL drops to 0.55/0.58/0.67/0.72\n- Table 3: RRF AUC=0.87, 2-signal=0.78\n- Table 6: Noise at 2×: CI→8.9pp, Cov→0.97, θ corr→0.89\n- Table 7: 7B MAE=2.2pp; 72B γ=0.18(MMLU)\n- Table 8: Signal×regime heatmap\n- Table 9: RL regret: uncalib=0.38, ContamCal=0.22 (42% reduction)\n- Table 11: Prior sensitivity: max MAE diff=0.13pp across 3 priors\n- Table 12: Few-examinee: N=50 w/ preestimate → MAE=2.1pp\n- Table 13: 2-signal mode MAE=2.4pp vs 4-signal 1.97pp\n\n## Experiment Design Coverage Check\nThe paper's imagined experiment plan included these experiments:\n- exp-001: Controlled SFT injection ✓ (covered)\n- exp-002: Stage 1 signal pipeline ✓ (covered)\n- exp-003: RRF aggregation ablation ✓ (covered)\n- exp-005: Noise propagation ✓ (covered)\n- exp-006: IRT calibration + baselines ✓ (covered as main result)\n- exp-007: 7B scale bridging ✓ (covered)\n- exp-008: Signal survival analysis ✓ (covered)\n- exp-009: RL observational case study ✓ (covered)\n- exp-010: Prior sensitivity ✓ (covered)\n- exp-011: Few-examinee regime ✓ (covered)\n- exp-012: 72B observational ✓ (covered)\n- exp-013: Closed-weight 2-signal ✓ (covered)\n\nAll 12 planned experiments are present in the paper. However, note that many tables are incomplete (showing \"—\" for most cells), which I'll ask you to evaluate.\n\n## YOUR TASK\n\nProvide a detailed review focused on:\n\n### A. Methodological Soundness\n1. Is the 4PL+γ formulation mathematically sound? Is a single global γ appropriate vs per-item or per-model γ?\n2. Is the RRF aggregation well-justified? Why not learned weights?\n3. Are the priors appropriate? Is the identifiability argument (θ-γ correlation=0.31 < 0.4) convincing?\n4. Is the measurement error extension properly formulated?\n5. The causal claim about \"counterfactual ability θ*\" — is this identification actually valid?\n\n### B. Experimental Rigor\n1. Is the controlled SFT injection protocol convincing as ground truth? Does paraphrase-based injection realistically simulate real-world contamination?\n2. Are the baselines comprehensive and fairly compared? Note that Table 4 shows \"—\" for per-benchmark breakdowns of all baselines.\n3. Are the evaluation metrics appropriate and sufficient?\n4. Is the scale bridging argument (7B controlled + 72B observational) convincing?\n5. Is the RL case study (observational only) strong enough for the claims made?\n6. Few-examinee study: is N≥50 with pre-estimation a practical requirement?\n\n### C. Presentation Quality\n1. Are the tables well-formatted? Note the excessive \"—\" entries.\n2. Are there missing figures that would strengthen the paper?\n3. Is the writing clear and well-structured?\n\n### D. Weaknesses and Questions for Authors\n\n### E. Overall Assessment\nProvide scores on the AAAI 1-10 scale:\n- Overall: X/10\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Significance: X/5\n- Novelty: X/5\n- Clarity: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n\nFormat your review as a structured reviewer report.\n","agentId":"ab004b25805546cbf","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have a comprehensive understanding of the paper, the experimental plan, the imagined results, and the MVP simulation evidence. Let me compose the review.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods and Experiments\n\n### Paper Summary\n\nContamCal proposes a two-stage post-hoc calibration framework for benchmark scores under training data contamination. Stage 1 aggregates four detection signals via Reciprocal Rank Fusion (RRF) into per-item exposure scores. Stage 2 fits a contamination-augmented 4PL IRT model via Bayesian MCMC, yielding calibrated ability estimates with credible intervals. The paper claims 1.97pp MAE with 93% CI coverage on controlled SFT injection experiments, 44% improvement over the best baseline, and 42% regret reduction for RL-trained models.\n\n---\n\n### A. Methodological Soundness\n\n**A1. 4PL+gamma formulation.**\nThe formulation in Eq. (2) is mathematically coherent: adding gamma * e_ji to the ability parameter theta_j inside the logistic link is a standard covariate-in-the-exponent approach from item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017). However, using a **single global gamma** is a strong and potentially problematic assumption. It asserts that one unit of exposure produces the same ability boost regardless of:\n- The model (a 0.5B model memorizing a MMLU fact vs. a 7B model)\n- The item type (MCQ knowledge recall vs. code generation)\n- The benchmark (MMLU vs. HumanEval)\n\nThe paper acknowledges that smaller models show larger inflation (10.8pp vs. 8.2pp at 50% dosage), yet the IRT model forces a single gamma across all models and benchmarks in each fit. If the model is fitted per-benchmark (the paper is ambiguous on this), then the cross-benchmark constraint is relaxed, but per-model heterogeneity in contamination susceptibility remains unmodeled. A hierarchical gamma (e.g., gamma_j ~ N(mu_gamma, sigma_gamma)) or at minimum benchmark-specific gammas would be more defensible. The paper should clarify the scope of each IRT fit and justify why per-model heterogeneity does not bias theta estimates.\n\n**A2. RRF aggregation.**\nThe meta-circularity argument against learned weights (Section 2.1 of the framing) is reasonable but somewhat hand-wavy. Learned weights could be trained on synthetic contamination (which the authors already produce for ground truth), avoiding the circularity concern. The actual reason RRF works well is probably that signal quality is sufficiently uniform in the SFT regime that rank-based fusion is near-optimal. The paper should acknowledge this and note that RRF's advantage is simplicity and robustness to outlier signal scores, not theoretical superiority. The ablation showing calibration-weighted RRF (AUC=0.86) slightly underperforms vanilla RRF (0.87) is interesting but only tested under best-case conditions.\n\n**A3. Priors and identifiability.**\nThe priors are generally reasonable for IRT (LogNormal(0,0.5) for discrimination, Beta(2,8)/(8,2) for guessing/ceiling). The identifiability argument rests on theta-gamma posterior correlation = 0.31, claimed to be below a \"0.4 threshold.\" This threshold appears ad hoc -- I am not aware of a principled identifiability threshold for posterior correlations in IRT models. The MVP simulation results (P2 confounding test) are more convincing: at theta-exposure confounding correlation = 0.38, the residual correlation drops to 0.058 after fitting, and gamma relative bias is 7.7%. However, the MVP was run at a single seed with N_items=300 and N_models=50. The imagined full sweep (exp-004) would strengthen this considerably, but **the paper presents this sweep's results without actually having run it** -- only the single MVP seed exists. This is the most critical gap.\n\n**A4. Measurement error extension.**\nThe formulation e_obs = e_true + epsilon is standard classical measurement error. However, the paper does not specify how e_true is marginalized out in the Stan model. In a proper Bayesian treatment, e_true would be a latent variable for each item, which dramatically increases the parameter space (adding ~N_items latent variables per model). If instead sigma_e is simply used to inflate gamma's posterior variance post-hoc, the uncertainty propagation is approximate and potentially incorrect. The DIC improvement of 12.3 suggests a genuine model, but the implementation details are crucial and missing.\n\n**A5. Counterfactual theta* identification.**\nThe causal claim is that theta_j estimated with gamma*e_ji subtracted represents the \"counterfactual ability\" -- what would be estimated absent contamination. This is valid **only under the assumption that contamination acts purely additively in the logit space and that the exposure scores are calibrated proxies for true exposure**. The additive assumption means contamination does not change the model's actual reasoning ability, only its performance on seen items. This is plausible for SFT memorization but less clear for RL, where training on contaminated data might genuinely improve reasoning. The paper should be more careful in distinguishing \"memorization-inflated performance\" from \"genuine learning from benchmark-adjacent data.\"\n\n---\n\n### B. Experimental Rigor\n\n**B1. Controlled SFT injection protocol.**\nParaphrase-based injection is a well-established protocol (Yang et al. 2023, Oren et al. 2024). The design -- 3 scales x 5 dosages x 4 benchmarks with 15 model pairs -- is solid for internal validity. The key question is ecological validity: real-world contamination involves pre-training data overlap, not post-hoc SFT injection. SFT injection produces a very specific contamination signature (explicit recent memorization during fine-tuning), which may be easier for detection signals to catch than pre-training contamination that has been partially overwritten by subsequent training. The paper should discuss this limitation more explicitly.\n\n**B2. Baselines: the \"---\" problem.**\nThis is a **major presentation weakness** that borders on a rigor concern. Table 4 (the main results table) shows per-benchmark MAE for ContamCal (1.8, 2.1, 1.6, 2.4) but \"---\" for all five baselines across all four benchmarks plus coverage, CI width, and tau. This means:\n- The reader cannot assess whether ContamCal's advantage is uniform across benchmarks or concentrated on a subset\n- Baseline CI coverage and width are unreported, making the uncertainty quantification comparison impossible\n- Kendall tau for baselines is unreported, preventing ranking comparison\n\nThe imagined experiment plan (exp-006) specifies that all baselines should be evaluated on all 5 metrics. The paper as written has **not filled in these numbers**. This would be a fatal flaw at review -- I would immediately desk-reject for incompleteness. Similarly, Tables 5 (per-benchmark detail), 6 (noise), 7 (scale), 11 (few-examinee), and others have extensive \"---\" entries for values that should be computable from the experiments described.\n\n**B3. Evaluation metrics.**\nThe 5 metrics are well-chosen: MAE (calibration), coverage at multiple levels (reliability), CI width (sharpness), Kendall tau (ranking), dose-response Spearman (sanity). The one metric I would add is **calibration slope** -- plot calibrated vs. true scores and measure slope. A method could achieve low MAE through a constant offset while having poor marginal calibration. Also, 50% coverage = 0.55 (from the imagined experiments) suggests slight miscalibration at the 50% level, which should be discussed.\n\n**B4. Scale bridging argument.**\nThe 7B controlled result (MAE=2.2pp) adds incremental confidence. The 72B observational analysis is inevitably weaker -- without ground truth, gamma=0.18 on MMLU could be correct or could reflect systematic bias in the detection signals at this scale. The fact that gamma CI includes zero on GSM8K is presented as \"consistent with reasoning benchmarks being less susceptible,\" but it could equally mean the method has insufficient power at 72B. The argument would be much stronger with a controlled 72B experiment (acknowledged as infeasible due to compute).\n\n**B5. RL case study.**\nThe RL case study is purely observational with 8 models. There is no ground truth for RL contamination, so the 42% regret reduction is computed against... what exactly? The regret calculation requires knowing the \"true\" uncontaminated ranking, but for RL models this is unknown. The paper appears to use the calibrated ranking as a proxy for ground truth when computing regret, which would be circular. If instead regret is computed using downstream task performance or held-out clean benchmarks as ground truth, this should be stated explicitly. As written, this is a significant methodological gap.\n\n**B6. Few-examinee study.**\nN>=50 with pre-estimation from an external pool of 400+ models is a practical but demanding requirement. The pre-estimation strategy effectively assumes that item parameters (a, b, c, d) are stable across different model pools, which may not hold if the evaluation pool has very different capability distributions. The paper should validate this transfer assumption.\n\n---\n\n### C. Presentation Quality\n\n**C1. Tables with \"---\" entries.**\nAs noted in B2, this is the single biggest presentation problem. Of the 14 tables in the paper, approximately 60% of cells that should contain values show \"---\" instead. This makes the paper look like an incomplete draft. The imagined experiments spec shows that all these values were planned to be reported. The effect on reviewers would be devastating -- it signals either that experiments were not actually run, or that unfavorable results are being hidden.\n\n**C2. Missing figures.**\nThe paper contains **zero figures**. The imagined experiment plan describes 21 figures including dose-response curves, scatter plots, heatmaps, bar charts, and coverage calibration plots. None appear in the submitted paper. For a methods paper of this complexity, at minimum these are essential:\n- A framework overview diagram (Stage 1 + Stage 2 pipeline)\n- Calibrated vs. true accuracy scatter plot with CI error bars (the \"money figure\")\n- Signal x regime AUC heatmap\n- Dose-response curves\n\nWithout these, the paper is extremely hard to evaluate visually.\n\n**C3. Writing quality.**\nThe writing is clear, well-organized, and appropriately concise. The introduction effectively motivates the problem and positions the work relative to detection-only and IRT-only approaches. The \"statistical safety net\" framing is compelling. Section 2 (Method) is technically clear. The three structural adaptations from educational IRT to LLM evaluation are well-articulated.\n\n---\n\n### D. Weaknesses and Questions for Authors\n\n**Major weaknesses:**\n\n1. **Incomplete tables (critical).** The overwhelming number of \"---\" entries in results tables makes the paper unacceptable for review in its current form. All baselines must have per-benchmark breakdowns, CI coverage, CI width, and ranking metrics.\n\n2. **No figures (critical).** A paper proposing a complex two-stage Bayesian pipeline with 12 experiments and 14 tables needs at least 4-5 figures. The absence of a single figure is disqualifying.\n\n3. **RL regret calculation may be circular.** How is ground truth defined for the expected regret computation on RL models? If the calibrated scores are used as ground truth, the 42% improvement is tautological.\n\n4. **Single global gamma.** The assumption that contamination has a uniform effect across models and items within a benchmark is strong and potentially violated by the paper's own observation of scale-dependent inflation.\n\n5. **MVP simulation is insufficient for the claims made.** The identifiability argument relies on a single MVP run (1 seed, 1 condition), yet the paper presents results from a comprehensive 144-condition sweep that was planned but may not have been executed. It is unclear which simulation results in the paper are from actual runs vs. imagined best-case outcomes.\n\n**Minor weaknesses:**\n\n6. **Measurement error model implementation details missing.** How are latent true exposure scores handled in the Stan model?\n\n7. **No error bars / standard deviations.** The experiment plan specifies 3 seeds for key experiments, but no standard deviations are reported anywhere in the paper.\n\n8. **Embedding retrieval requires training corpus access.** This is acknowledged implicitly (closed-weight 2-signal mode) but not discussed as a limitation of the full pipeline.\n\n9. **ConStat requires paraphrase generation.** Who generates the paraphrases for ConStat, and could the paraphrase quality itself introduce bias?\n\n**Questions for authors:**\n\nQ1. Is the IRT model fitted jointly across all benchmarks with a single gamma, or separately per benchmark? The paper is ambiguous.\n\nQ2. For the RL expected regret analysis (Table 9), what serves as ground truth for model ranking? How do you know the calibrated ranking is \"better\"?\n\nQ3. The 0.4 threshold for posterior correlation as an identifiability criterion -- what is the theoretical or empirical basis for this specific value?\n\nQ4. How does the measurement error model handle the latent true exposure scores in Stan? Are they fully marginalized, treated as latent variables with MCMC, or handled via an approximation?\n\nQ5. Table 8 (signal x regime) shows embedding retrieval AUC = 0.90 for \"Base\" models. Why would base models (no contamination) show high retrieval AUC? Does this indicate that the embedding signal has high false positive rates on base models?\n\nQ6. The paper reports 50% coverage = 0.55 (from the experiment plan). This exceeds the nominal 50% -- does this indicate the CIs are slightly too wide? Have you considered calibrating the CI widths themselves?\n\n---\n\n### E. Overall Assessment\n\n**Summary judgment:** The core idea is sound and addresses a genuine gap -- no existing method provides calibrated benchmark scores under contamination with proper uncertainty quantification. The cross-disciplinary transfer from educational measurement's item compromise IRT is well-motivated, and the three structural adaptations are non-trivial. The experimental design is comprehensive on paper. However, the submitted manuscript is **clearly incomplete**: pervasive \"---\" entries in tables, zero figures, no error bars, and a potentially circular RL regret calculation. If these issues were resolved, this could be a strong AAAI paper. In its current form, it reads as a detailed experimental plan with a well-written methods section, not a completed study.\n\n**Scores (AAAI 1-10 scale):**\n\n| Criterion | Score | Justification |\n|-----------|-------|---------------|\n| **Overall** | **4/10** | Strong idea, comprehensive plan, but manuscript is incomplete -- missing figures, pervasive \"---\" in tables, no error bars. Cannot evaluate the actual empirical strength. |\n| **Soundness** | **3/5** | Core formulation is correct. Single global gamma, ad hoc identifiability threshold, and potentially circular RL regret are concerns. Measurement error implementation unclear. |\n| **Significance** | **4/5** | High practical value. Contamination calibration with UQ is a genuine unsolved problem. If validated, would be widely adopted. |\n| **Novelty** | **3/5** | Synthesis of existing components (IRT + detection signals + RRF) with meaningful adaptations. The cross-disciplinary transfer is the main novelty. Individual components are not new. |\n| **Clarity** | **2/5** | Writing quality is good, but the manuscript is incomplete. Zero figures and heavily sparse tables make evaluation impossible. |\n| **Confidence** | **4/5** | I am familiar with IRT, Bayesian methods, and LLM contamination literature. My concerns are grounded in methodological standards for this type of work. |\n\n**Recommendation:** Reject in current form. Resubmit after: (1) completing all table entries with actual experimental results and standard deviations, (2) adding at minimum 5 key figures, (3) clarifying the RL regret ground truth, (4) justifying or relaxing the single global gamma assumption, (5) providing measurement error model implementation details. The underlying research direction is promising and could reach acceptance at a top venue with thorough execution."}],"totalDurationMs":167250,"totalTokens":71876,"totalToolUseCount":19,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2032,"cache_read_input_tokens":65984,"output_tokens":3859,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2032},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3859,"cache_read_input_tokens":65984,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2032,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2032,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":3,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":10}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"a5ac0a7826a71f52a","tool_use_id":"toolu_01F7xAjPguEspUEZA7biSaVE","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","usage":{"total_tokens":81992,"tool_uses":21,"duration_ms":192174},"last_tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","uuid":"f04a91f6-2795-461a-b693-6e7221f22a1c","session_id":"12c88d9d-1477-4077-a4d3-f782256d68e2"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01ALZMcDVWifoX3G7W9bCbjj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RKvvBFcwgUf77hu7hsYvAv","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"project":"contamcal-irt-calibration","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260513T155040","overall_score":"borderline accept","report":"\n# Reviewer 2 (R2) — Novelty and Related Work\n\n**Paper:** ContamCal: Calibrating Benchmark Scores for Training Data Contamination via Contamination-Augmented Item Response Theory\n\n**Venue:** AAAI 2027 (acceptance rate ~17.6%)\n\n---\n\n## A. Novelty Assessment\n\n### A1. Core idea: contamination as covariate rather than removable confound\n\nThis is the paper's strongest conceptual contribution. The shift from \"detect and remove\" to \"detect and adjust\" is genuinely well-motivated, especially given the Emperor's New Clothes result (Dong et al., ICML 2025) showing all 20 mitigation strategies fail. The causal inference analogy (eliminating confounders vs. adjusting for them) is apt and provides solid intellectual grounding.\n\nHowever, I note that this framing is more of a *paradigm restatement* than a *technical invention*. The item compromise IRT literature (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) already does exactly this in educational testing — the paper's contribution is recognizing that this existing paradigm applies to LLM evaluation. This is valuable cross-disciplinary transfer, but the novelty ceiling is lower than the paper implies. The paper should more explicitly acknowledge that the core modeling idea (exposure covariate in IRT) is borrowed wholesale from educational measurement, and that the contribution lies in the adaptation and validation for the LLM setting.\n\n**Novelty: Moderate.** The insight is important but not technically novel — it is an application of an established idea to a new domain.\n\n### A2. Three structural modifications from educational IRT\n\nThe paper claims three non-trivial adaptations:\n\n**(a) Independent examinees to single model with joint item distributions.** This is the most technically interesting adaptation. In educational testing, each examinee is independent; in LLM evaluation, a single model produces all responses, changing the local independence assumption. However, the paper does not deeply engage with the statistical consequences. Does this violate IRT's local independence assumption? If so, how severe is the violation, and what are the consequences for posterior inference? The paper treats this as a solved problem by simply fitting the standard model, but the theoretical implications deserve more attention. This modification is stated rather than developed.\n\n**(b) Binary leakage to continuous exposure scores.** This is a natural and relatively straightforward generalization. The educational IRT literature already uses graded exposure indicators (e.g., partial item compromise models). The paper's use of continuous RRF scores is a reasonable choice but not a fundamentally new modeling idea.\n\n**(c) Stable ability to regime-conditional ability (SFT vs. RL).** This is primarily an engineering decision about when to use which signals, not a deep structural modification to the IRT model itself. The model does not actually have a regime-conditional ability parameter — rather, it uses different signal subsets depending on the detected training regime. This is signal selection, not a new IRT formulation.\n\n**Novelty: Low-to-Moderate.** Adaptation (a) has theoretical depth that is under-explored; (b) is incremental; (c) is engineering rather than modeling.\n\n### A3. RRF fusion of detection signals\n\nRRF (Cormack et al., 2009) is a well-established information retrieval technique. Applying it to aggregate contamination detection signals is a sensible engineering choice but carries no methodological novelty. The paper's own ablation shows that RRF barely outperforms the best single signal in terms of AUC (0.87 vs 0.88 in the best tier), with its advantage lying in robustness across benchmarks rather than peak performance. The choice of c=60 is the standard default. No novel fusion methodology is proposed.\n\n**Novelty: Low.** Straightforward application of existing technique.\n\n### A4. Regime-conditional modeling / auto-degradation for RL\n\nThis is an engineering decision, not a methodological contribution. The system detects which signals are available and uses them — there is no novel statistical machinery for handling regime shifts. A genuine contribution here would be a formal model that jointly infers the training regime and adjusts accordingly, or a principled Bayesian approach to signal selection. Instead, the paper uses a fixed heuristic (if RL-trained, drop embedding and Min-K++).\n\n**Novelty: Low.** Engineering heuristic rather than methodological contribution.\n\n### A5. Incremental novelty over \"detection + remove-and-rescore\"\n\nThe key advantage of ContamCal over remove-and-rescore is twofold: (1) it uses continuous exposure scores rather than binary flags, preserving contamination degree information; and (2) it produces calibrated scores with credible intervals rather than simple adjusted point estimates. The MAE improvement (1.97pp vs 4.2pp for remove-and-rescore in the best tier) is substantial. The uncertainty quantification via Bayesian posterior is a genuine practical advantage.\n\nHowever, the comparison to linear regression + bootstrap CI (3.5pp MAE) is more informative. The 44% improvement over this baseline comes from the IRT framework's nonlinear item response modeling, not from the contamination covariate per se. This raises the question: how much of the improvement is from 4PL IRT vs. from the contamination-specific augmentation? The ablation (vanilla 4PL at 6.1pp vs. 4PL+gamma at 1.97pp) answers this: the contamination covariate is essential. But the comparison to linear regression suggests that a simpler contamination-aware model (linear exposure adjustment with bootstrap) captures much of the value.\n\n**Novelty: Moderate.** The combination provides clear empirical value, but the individual components are all known.\n\n### A. Overall Novelty Assessment\n\nContamCal is a *synthesis* paper — it combines existing detection signals (known), RRF fusion (known), 4PL IRT (known), contamination covariates in IRT (known from educational measurement), and Bayesian inference via Stan NUTS (known). The novelty lies in the *combination* and the *validation* in the LLM evaluation setting. This is a legitimate form of contribution, but reviewers at AAAI should be aware that no individual technical component is new. The paper's value is primarily empirical and practical rather than methodological.\n\n**Overall Novelty Score: 3/5** — Moderate. Cross-disciplinary synthesis with some novel validation, but no new techniques.\n\n---\n\n## B. Related Work Completeness\n\n### B1. Overall comprehensiveness\n\nThe related work section is **significantly underdeveloped**. With only 10 citations in the best-tier paper, this is far below the standard for a top venue. A typical AAAI paper in this area would cite 30-50 works. The related work section (Section 5 in best-tier) is approximately half a page — inadequate for a paper that spans contamination detection, IRT, Bayesian inference, and evaluation methodology.\n\n### B2. Missing related work areas\n\n**(i) Additional contamination detection methods.** The paper uses 4 signals but the detection literature is much broader:\n- Extraction attacks (Carlini et al., 2021; \"Extracting training data from large language models\")\n- Membership inference attacks (Mattern et al., 2023; Shi et al., 2023 beyond Min-K%)\n- KDS (Knowledge-Data Separation) is mentioned in the paper framing but absent from the paper itself\n- Golchin & Surdeanu (2024) — time travel contamination detection\n- Oren et al. (2024) — \"Proving test set contamination in black-box language models\" (cited but only in context of Emperor's, not as a detection method)\n- The paper should discuss why these 4 signals were chosen over alternatives\n\n**(ii) Other IRT applications in NLP/ML.**\n- HiBayES (Paganini 2024) is mentioned in the framing but absent from the best-tier paper — this is a significant omission as it directly addresses Bayesian uncertainty in LLM evaluation\n- DCR (Yang et al., 2023) — Data Contamination Report, which does perform score adjustment — is mentioned in the framing but absent from the paper. This is a critical missing baseline/comparison\n- PSN-IRT is mentioned in the paper framing but uses a different citation style\n\n**(iii) Post-hoc calibration methods from the broader ML literature.**\n- Platt scaling, temperature scaling, and isotonic regression are standard post-hoc calibration methods. While they address probability calibration rather than score calibration, the conceptual connection should be discussed — and the paper should explain why these are not applicable or relevant\n- Conformal prediction methods for uncertainty quantification — an alternative to Bayesian CIs that the paper should at least acknowledge\n\n**(iv) Score adjustment and fairness calibration.**\n- DIF (Differential Item Functioning) literature in educational testing — directly relevant as it addresses systematic score differences across subgroups, analogous to contaminated vs. clean models\n- Test equating methods in psychometrics\n\n**(v) Benchmark design and dynamic benchmarks.**\n- LiveBench (White et al., 2024) — dynamic benchmarks that avoid contamination by construction\n- SEAL (Scale et al., 2024) — contamination-resistant evaluation\n- The paper should discuss how ContamCal relates to the \"prevention vs. cure\" spectrum — dynamic benchmarks prevent contamination, ContamCal corrects for it post-hoc\n\n**(vi) Data provenance and attribution.**\n- Data attribution methods (Ilyas et al., 2022; Park et al., 2023) that trace model behavior to training data — conceptually related to Stage 1's provenance mapping\n- The connection to influence functions and training data attribution should be discussed\n\n### B3. Connection to educational measurement's item compromise IRT\n\nThe connection to Eckerly (2017) and Sinharay (2017) is stated but not developed. The paper should:\n- Explain the specific models used in these works (what kind of exposure covariate? binary or graded?)\n- Discuss the empirical findings from educational testing — how well does item compromise IRT work in that setting? What are the known limitations?\n- Cite additional work in this tradition (e.g., McLeod et al., 2003; Segall, 2004 on item exposure control; Zhang, 2014 on person-fit with compromised items)\n- The adaptation from human testing (thousands of independent examinees) to LLM evaluation (tens of correlated models) is the paper's key theoretical bridge and deserves deeper treatment\n\n### B4. Concurrent/recent works (2025-2026)\n\nGiven the rapid pace of the field, several recent works are likely relevant:\n- The paper cites Dong et al. (ICML 2025) but should check for responses/follow-ups to this work\n- Any 2025-2026 work on contamination-aware evaluation or contamination quantification\n- Recent IRT-based LLM evaluation work beyond the three cited (TinyBenchmarks, Growing Pains, ATLAS)\n- The growing literature on evaluation methodology for LLMs (e.g., Liang et al., HELM; Biderman et al., lessons from evaluation)\n\n**Related Work Assessment: Major weakness.** The section needs substantial expansion — at minimum doubling the citation count and adding discussion of the areas identified above.\n\n---\n\n## C. Positioning and Framing\n\n### C1. \"Statistical safety net\" framing\n\nThe framing is effective and well-motivated. The progression from \"mitigation doesn't work\" (Emperor's New Clothes) to \"calibration as the alternative\" is logical and compelling. The causal inference analogy is well-chosen. However, the \"safety net\" metaphor slightly oversells — a safety net implies catching you reliably, but the RL case study shows the method's limitations are significant for the models practitioners most care about.\n\n### C2. Distinction from prior work\n\nThe paper distinguishes itself well from detection methods (which don't calibrate) and IRT methods (which don't model contamination). The distinction from remove-and-rescore is clear and empirically supported. However, the distinction from DCR (Data Contamination Report) is absent — this is the most directly comparable prior work and must be discussed.\n\n### C3. Scope of claims\n\nThe claims in the best-tier paper are ambitious but supported within the controlled SFT setting. The headline \"1.97pp MAE with 93% CI coverage\" is compelling. However, I have concerns:\n- The 44% improvement over \"best baseline\" is measured against linear regression, not against a method that was specifically designed for this task. The baselines are somewhat strawman-ish — four of five are simple ablations or known-ineffective methods (Emperor's mitigations).\n- The \"best\" tier imagined results are extremely optimistic. The median tier (4.2pp MAE, 89% coverage) and worst tier (7.8pp MAE, 78% coverage) paint a more realistic picture. The gap between best and worst is enormous — 4x in MAE.\n- The gap between best-tier dream (signal AUC 0.88 for embedding retrieval) and worst-tier dream (AUC 0.72) suggests that everything hinges on detection signal quality. If the signals work well, the method works; if they don't, it doesn't. This makes ContamCal's contribution somewhat parasitic on the quality of upstream detection.\n\n---\n\n## D. Significance and Impact\n\n### D1. Impact for AAAI\n\nThe paper addresses a timely and important problem. AAAI reviewers in the AI evaluation track would find this relevant. The Bayesian methodology is well-suited to AAAI's strengths. The paper would benefit from:\n- Framing as evaluation methodology (AAAI strength) rather than NLP-specific\n- Emphasizing the decision-theoretic contribution (expected regret analysis) which aligns with AAAI's decision-making tradition\n- The identifiability analysis (better developed in the median/worst tiers) is actually a strong methodological contribution that the best tier underplays\n\n### D2. Problem importance\n\nContamination is a genuine and growing concern, but reasonable people disagree about its severity relative to other evaluation problems (benchmark saturation, evaluation gaming, construct validity). The paper's opening claim that contamination inflates scores by \"8-15pp\" is from specific studies and may not generalize. The paper should acknowledge that for many practical use cases (e.g., choosing between GPT-4o and Claude Opus), contamination may not be the dominant source of evaluation error.\n\n### D3. Practical adoption barriers\n\nSeveral barriers would limit adoption:\n1. **Computational cost:** Stan NUTS with 4 chains x 4000 iterations per benchmark is non-trivial. The paper does not report wall-clock time.\n2. **Signal computation:** Computing 4 detection signals per (model, item) pair requires model weights (for 2 of 4 signals), training data access (for embedding retrieval), and paraphrase generation (for ConStat). This is a substantial infrastructure requirement.\n3. **Model pool requirement:** The N>=50 models requirement means this is only applicable to large-scale evaluation efforts (e.g., leaderboards), not individual model evaluations.\n4. **RL limitation:** The method's limited effectiveness for RL-trained models is a serious practical barrier, as most production models use RL post-training.\n\n---\n\n## E. Weaknesses and Questions for Authors\n\n### Major Weaknesses\n\n**W1. Novelty is primarily synthesis, not innovation.** Every component (RRF, 4PL IRT, contamination covariates, Bayesian MCMC) is borrowed from existing work. The cross-disciplinary transfer from educational measurement is valuable but the paper does not adequately develop the theoretical implications of the adaptations.\n\n**W2. Related work is critically insufficient.** 10 citations is unacceptable for a top venue paper spanning this many subfields. Missing comparisons to DCR, HiBayES, DIF, conformal prediction, dynamic benchmarks, and the broader contamination detection literature significantly weaken the paper's positioning.\n\n**W3. The \"best tier\" results may be unrealistically optimistic.** Comparing across dream tiers: 1.97pp MAE (best) vs 4.2pp (median) vs 7.8pp (worst) — a 4x range. The key driver is detection signal quality, which is assumed rather than established. If real detection signals perform closer to the worst tier (which the project's own simulation study suggests at N_items=300), the practical value diminishes substantially.\n\n**W4. Controlled SFT injection may not generalize to real-world contamination.** SFT injection via paraphrasing is one contamination mechanism, but real-world contamination includes: verbatim memorization during pretraining, indirect contamination via instruction tuning on benchmark-derived data, contamination via synthetic data that was itself generated using benchmark items. The paper validates on one mechanism and claims generality.\n\n**W5. The RL regime limitation is understated.** Most models that practitioners actually want to evaluate (instruction-tuned, RLHF-trained) fall in the RL regime where the method's detection signals degrade. The best tier claims Self-Critique AUC of 0.72 under RL, but the worst tier shows 0.57. If the true AUC is closer to 0.57, the method provides essentially no useful calibration for the models that matter most.\n\n### Minor Weaknesses\n\n**W6.** The choice of c=60 in RRF is the standard default but is not justified for this specific application. A sensitivity analysis on c would strengthen the paper.\n\n**W7.** The paper uses a global gamma parameter for contamination effect. This assumes homogeneous contamination effects across items within a benchmark, which is unrealistic — some items may be more memorizable than others. An item-specific or at least item-category-specific gamma would be more realistic.\n\n**W8.** The 72B observational analysis lacks ground truth and provides only weak evidence. The gamma=0.18 on MMLU \"suggesting detectable contamination\" is not validated — it could also reflect model specification error.\n\n**W9.** Local independence assumption. The paper does not discuss whether the standard IRT local independence assumption holds when all responses come from the same model (or a family of closely related models). This is a fundamental modeling concern.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n**Q1.** How does ContamCal compare to DCR (Yang et al., 2023), which also provides contamination-adjusted scores? Why is DCR not included as a baseline?\n\n**Q2.** What is the wall-clock computational cost of the full pipeline (4 signals + IRT fitting) for a single benchmark?\n\n**Q3.** Can you provide evidence that the local independence assumption is approximately satisfied in your setting? What is the residual correlation structure after conditioning on theta and gamma?\n\n**Q4.** The paper assumes a single global gamma. Have you considered item-level or item-cluster-level contamination effects? What happens if contamination is heterogeneous across items?\n\n**Q5.** How sensitive are results to the choice of RRF constant c=60?\n\n---\n\n## F. Overall Assessment\n\n### Scores (AAAI 1-10 scale)\n\n- **Overall: 5/10** — Borderline. The problem is important and the approach is sensible, but novelty is limited to cross-disciplinary synthesis, and the related work section is critically underdeveloped. The best-tier results are compelling but may be optimistic relative to achievable signal quality.\n\n- **Soundness: 3/5** — The Bayesian IRT framework is technically sound. The controlled SFT injection provides valid internal evaluation. However, the local independence assumption is unexamined, the generalizability beyond SFT contamination is uncertain, and the gap between best/median/worst tier projections raises questions about robustness.\n\n- **Significance: 3/5** — The problem is timely and the \"calibration not elimination\" paradigm shift is valuable. However, practical adoption barriers (signal infrastructure, model pool requirements, RL limitations) limit near-term impact. The expected regret analysis for decision-making is a nice contribution.\n\n- **Novelty: 2/5** — The individual components are all borrowed from existing work. The contribution is synthesis and domain adaptation, not new methodology. The three claimed structural adaptations from educational IRT are either under-developed (local independence), incremental (continuous exposure), or engineering (regime selection).\n\n- **Clarity: 4/5** — The best-tier paper is well-written with clear problem formulation, clean notation, and good experimental structure. Tables are informative. The \"wide CIs as the correct answer\" framing for the RL case is effective communication.\n\n- **Confidence: 4/5** — I am familiar with IRT, contamination detection, and Bayesian inference. I have moderate familiarity with the specific educational measurement item compromise literature.\n\n### Summary Recommendation\n\nThe paper presents a well-engineered synthesis that addresses an important problem. The core insight — calibrate rather than eliminate — is sound and well-motivated. However, the novelty bar for AAAI 2027 requires more than combining known components, and the critically thin related work section would be a red flag for any reviewer. The authors should: (1) substantially expand related work to 30+ citations, (2) more honestly position the novelty as cross-disciplinary transfer rather than implying new methodology, (3) better develop the theoretical implications of adapting educational IRT to the single-model setting, and (4) include DCR and HiBayES as baselines/comparisons. If these issues are addressed and the experimental results hold closer to the median tier (4.2pp MAE, 89% coverage) than the best tier, this could be a solid contribution. In its current form, I lean borderline accept/weak reject at AAAI.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":680,"cache_read_input_tokens":80544,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":680,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01F7xAjPguEspUEZA7biSaVE","session_id":"12c88d9d-1477-4077-a4d3-f782256d68e2","uuid":"9a788212-d7b5-4cf1-8b1b-16c3778cf78f"}
